{
 "id": "l35",
 "label": "35",
 "title": "Conjunctions and interjections",
 "summary": "Joining words such as at, o, péro, kung, nang, kundî, dáhil sa, sapagká't, and emotion words such as Abá!, Aráy!, Nakú! and Sáyang!",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Understand what conjunctions and interjections do",
  "Join words and ideas with at, o, péro and nguni't",
  "Say if, while and so that with kung (pag), samantalá (habang) and nang",
  "Say except, because of, because and therefore",
  "Use kung anó … siyá rin and at sa wakás",
  "Show feelings with Ay!, Abá!, Aráy!, Nakú!, Sáyang! and Mabúhay!"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Joining words and showing feelings",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "This lesson covers two small but very important kinds of words."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Kind",
      "What it does"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "**Conjunctions**",
       "join two clauses, phrases or words to show how they are related"
      ],
      [
       "**Interjections**",
       "are added to a sentence to show a particular emotion"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Two kinds of words"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A conjunction shows the relationship between the parts it joins. It points out that one thought (in a word, phrase or clause) is **less important than**, or **as important as**, the other one."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "In English",
     "t": "English conjunctions are words like *and*, *or*, *but*, *if* and *because*. English interjections are words like *Oh!* and *Ouch!*"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "The most common one",
     "t": "The most common Tagalog conjunction is [[at|other]], which means **and**. You met it in Lesson 5."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "And, or, but",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "1. at: and"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "[[at|other]] means **and**. When the word before it **ends in a vowel**, at may be shortened to **'t** and joined to that word."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Nagsúsulat siyá, gabí at áraw.",
       "en": "He writes night and day.",
